camchrome: QUIC (HTTP/3) over a SOCKS5 proxy's UDP relay; named relay proxies; no private relaying

Stock Chromium never speaks QUIC through a SOCKS5 proxy. The alternative (h3) job refuses any
non-QUIC proxy with ERR_NO_SUPPORTED_PROXIES and the request stays on HTTP/2 over the TCP tunnel,
so browserleaks.com/quic reports QUIC false and every Alt-Svc h3 origin is reached differently
from a real Chrome on a residential line. With --socks5-udp (opt-in, as for 973):

  * HttpStreamFactory::Job lets a QUIC job run when the chain is exactly one SOCKS5 proxy, and
    --origin-to-force-quic-on honours such a chain (HttpNetworkSession::ShouldForceQuic).
  * QuicSessionPool routes that chain through DirectJob (never AsyncDnsJob) and keeps the SOCKS5
    chain in the session key, so proxied and direct sessions never mix, and every migration path
    (port migration, network change, server preferred address) stays off: QuicChromiumClientSession
    already refuses those for proxied sessions. The multi-port probe socket, which had no such
    guard and would have gone straight to the origin, is declined for proxied sessions.
  * DirectJob does not resolve the origin. It connects to a placeholder address in 198.18.0.0/15
    that names the host, and SOCKS5UDPClientSocket sends every datagram with ATYP=DOMAINNAME so the
    proxy resolves it, exactly as SOCKS5 CONNECT does for TCP. No local DNS query for the origin.
  * SOCKS5UDPClientSocket (net/socket/socks5_udp_relay.*) is a connected DatagramClientSocket on
    top of 973's SOCKS5UDPSocket. ConnectAsync() completes only once the association is up, so a
    proxy that refuses UDP ASSOCIATE fails the QUIC job and the request carries on over TCP, as on
    a network that blocks UDP. A datagram with framing we cannot parse is skipped rather than
    reported (QUIC treats a read error as fatal for the session).
  * The QUIC socket never falls back to a direct one.

973 follow-ups in the same files:

  * A NAMED proxy is relayed. SOCKS5UDPSocket gained a constructor that resolves the proxy before
    opening the control connection. Before, a hostname in --proxy-server made the P2P factory hand
    WebRTC a DIRECT socket. --proxy-server is now parsed with Chromium's own proxy URI parser, so
    bracketed IPv6 literals work too.
  * The relay never delivers to private, loopback or link-local destinations (a peer's LAN host
    candidate would otherwise probe the proxy's own network). Those datagrams are reported sent and
    dropped, so ICE sees an unreachable candidate rather than a socket error. A proxy on this
    machine's loopback (test infrastructure) is exempt.
  * Replies addressed with ATYP=DOMAINNAME are accepted.

net/socket/socks5_udp_relay_selftest.cc (target socks5_udp_relay_selftest) checks the placeholder
mapping, chain selection, named-origin and named-proxy round trips, the queued synchronous path,
and that a refusing or unreachable proxy fails the connect, against tools/socks5_udp_proxy.py.
